From 97b583de92a37c6ac0ecf3732b807feee5cac6e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Evinj <natively.contact@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 10:27:51 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] feat(launcher): lock the launcher to 3:2, floor it at
 1200x800, animate maximize
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The launcher window is now shape-locked to 3:2 at every size, with a 1200x800
minimum, and its maximize/restore is animated by us instead of by the OS.

3:2 SHAPE LOCK
setAspectRatio(3/2) constrains user drags natively (verified on Windows: a real
corner drag held 3:2 across 135/135 frames, max deviation 0.0017). That is only
half the job — Electron does not apply the constraint to programmatic sizing,
and MEASURED on Windows 11, a native maximize still lands 1536x864 (16:9), while
maximizable:false does not block it. So every size this process chooses comes
from launcherAspect.ts, and enforceLauncherAspectRatio() corrects anything the
OS sets directly (Win+Up, title-bar double-click, Aero Snap).

1200x800 MINIMUM
Minimum equals the default, so the launcher only scales up. Both are derived
from the ratio: a min-size that is not 3:2 fights the aspect lock at the corner,
because the OS clamps to the min box and that clamp then violates the ratio. On
a work area smaller than 1200x800 the floor wins and the window is larger than
the work area, which is the intended trade.

MAXIMIZE FILLS THE SCREEN
The in-app maximize button is the one sanctioned exemption from 3:2: it fills
the work area at the display's own shape. It is a setBounds, not a native
maximize — this window is transparent (layered), and Windows composites the
animated maximize of a layered window badly. A flag tracks the fill state, since
the OS has no "maximized" state to read back. Every other route to a big window
still snaps to 3:2.

ANIMATION
Maximize/restore steps the frame along cubic-bezier(0.2, 0, 0, 1) — the Material
3 "emphasized" token, specified for a surface that transforms while staying on
screen. Durations are Fluent 2 tokens: 300ms (durationSlow) to expand, 250ms
(durationGentle) to contract, following both systems' rule that larger elements
get more time and the reverse direction is quicker.

The 8ms tick is measured, not guessed: Node timers are not vsync-aligned, so a
16ms tick that arrives late is a dropped frame. Over a maximize, gaps ran
15-32ms at a 16ms tick versus 12-22ms at 8ms. 4ms fires faster than a 60Hz
display can present. Per-frame resize bookkeeping stands down for the duration
and runs once on settle. The OS reduce-motion setting is honoured, which also
covers remote desktop sessions.

Also removes a diagnostic switch (NATIVELY_DISABLE_WINDOW_ANIMATIONS) that was
swept into 367913d8 while this work was in progress; it was never meant to ship.

Validation:
- Tested physically on Windows: corner drag, native maximize correction, Aero
  Snap correction, minimum-size floor, button fill/restore, animation cadence.
- Covered by automated macOS and Windows branch tests: the new util modules are
  pure math with no process.platform, so both branches run on either OS.
- Requires physical macOS verification: the green-button zoom goes through
  AppKit and never reaches maximizeWindow().
- tsc --noEmit clean; 164/164 electron/utils tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
